duced in a  way that includes I'm I'm thinking that  they include scripts and sort of some  pretty specific thought as to what is  said and when sometimes costumes and  imagery and and things that sort of go  around the edges of the ideas is that  something you do merely because you  enjoy it is there some strategy behind  it I mean speculating the idea that  maybe helps appeal to people that might  be I don't know center-right gamers that  might get pulled in by the algorithm or  whatever the case may be I mean how much  of that is merely what you like to do  versus a strategic decision I mean it's  both so but perhaps for your viewers who  haven't seen any of my videos I should  say I will sometimes include I use a lot  of visual metaphor these a lot of  costumes and characters and narrative  devices in my video about abortion which  we mentioned earlier it was spliced in  with a short film that I made and and I  do all of that again both I partly  because I love it because as in my other  life I am a professional actor and Idol  I love theater and film and the use of  visual metaphor and telling a story that  way and but it also does help it does  help draw people and people get excited  about it people like it it's very  different it's something that not a lot  of people can do you know when I started  out on YouTube  nobody was summarizing Plato but now you  throw a stone in the woods and that  you'll hit 50 youtubers summarizing  Plato right so incorporating all that  stuff is kind of like something that  only that only I can do and and so it  brings my unique creative twist to it  which I love and and it does it does  help people learn in different ways  because there's more than one way of  skinning the cat you know sometimes  people will say I don't really get what  you mean by and by Timothy Morton's  concept of the hyper object of climate  change and I said well it's in front of  you because that's the costume I'm  wearing is like a visual representation  of his whole book on this philosophy so  maybe if people find it hard to engage  one way the quote theatrical stuff can  help do it the other way like I did a  video a while ago honor the philosophy  of mind and trauma and it's all one  uncut half-hour monologue there's no  cuts to it and that's not an accident  that's not just I'm rambling it's very  tightly scripted it's because that is  actually designed to tell you something  about what it's like to learn about  trauma and to learn about these ideas so  I do it because I love it and because it  works and because it's just yeah it's so  much fun if it helps people learn is  there a particular philosophical thought  line that you think is most acutely  missing from the sort of brains of  people that are discussing politics and  culture and the way that society should  be organized where if it was more known  to people we would all be far better off  so like I often talk about how there's  such a lack of critical thinking and  sort of learning how to learn and  understanding how propaganda works and  that that's like a set of knowledge that  would be very useful in general for a  lot of the conversations we're trying to  have when it comes to philosophy is  there something in particular that you  believe if more people studied and were  acquainted with we would be much better  position to figure out many of the  problems that modern societies deal with  I definitely agree with you about  propaganda and then it's it's it's  useful to learn how that works I guess  something that I then I have struggled  to find a way to teach um is not even an  idea or a set of ideas but more like a  set of skills that studying philosophy  formerly brought me which is like how to  read and break down an argument so  sometimes I will get comments under my  videos where people say well what about  this thing this one tiny thing that you  said invalidates everything else and I  go well no because that's actually not  really relevant that was a side comment  or that was an aside or that's not  actually if you take that out the rest  of it still works so there's something  about argumentative structure and like  logical structure that I have I think  picked up it's it's not not even a  language like almost a grammar of  philosophical arguments which I have  struggled to find a way to teach on its  own and then I'm always kind of thinking  about which which I think may be maybe  useful and it's it's not even like  reading comprehension it's something  even more basic now logical  comprehension and like something being  entailed by something else and like how  arguments fit together  I think would be really I I would find  it very useful but then perhaps that's  just me not knowing how to effectively  communicate with people who haven't had  that that study yeah I mean it is it is  a skill in and of itself how to teach  people how to teach themselves so to  speak or to teach people how to learn  and I 100% agree I mean both in my  academic experience and my media  experience it just seems acutely missing  from a lot of the conversations that  that were having about so many of the  most important issues of the day we've  been speaking with Oliver thorn who is  not only a professional actor but also  the creator of philosophy tube on  YouTube Oliver so great to speak to you  I really appreciate your time my  pleasure thank you so much for having me  this is so much fun
 

